Neither do most players, as long as you warn them ahead of time.

> A much more valid point would be: "They are abusing the players as
> idea-cows, without having them participate in the decision process."

Well, yes.  That happens no matter what, with the players of any game;
it's just whether the designers acknowledge it openly or not.

It's hard to consider it abuse, since players will offer ideas
regardless of development method.  If some of those ideas happen to be
something the designers/coders like, well, congratulations!  But this is
hardly abuse.

> While I would not phrase it like that, this is much closer to the
> truth: we (the team) look at the proposals with voracious interest. We
> discuss, adapt and take the ideas we like; and we comment on and skip
> others (even if they are good, there may be several reasons not to
> implement some). And of course, there is no committee. Design is not
> decided in a democratic process.

Let me echo this for a second.

At the end of the day, I'm responsible for everything that goes into
SporkHack.  There are no other coders, though I am using Pasi Kallinen's 
new level compiler and have included a couple other small patches.  

Given that I have all the responsibility, I am also the final arbiter 
of what goes in.  Trying to pretend there was some democratic
process would be foolish, and trying to artificially create one would be
even more so; "Design by Committee" fails miserably, with its end result
invariably being either no results whatsoever, or utter garbage.

That doesn't mean I don't listen to my players very closely -- I do.  
But eventually, someone has to make a decision, and that's going to be 
me, and I'm going to do what I think is best for the game.

